    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\sin\\left(\\frac{A+B}{2}\\right)=\\cos\\frac C2$.\n\nUse $A+B=180^\\circ-C$ and the complementary angle identity.",
      "worked": "The angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, so $A+B=180^\\circ-C$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin\\left(\\frac{A+B}{2}\\right)&=\\sin\\left(\\frac{180^\\circ-C}{2}\\right)\\\\\\sin\\left(\\frac{A+B}{2}\\right)&=\\sin\\left(90^\\circ-\\frac C2\\right)\\\\\\sin\\left(\\frac{A+B}{2}\\right)&=\\cos\\frac C2.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 36
